hey David is it transphobic to be  annoyed by people who declare their  gender pronouns unprovoked and when it  seems irrelevant this is a very loaded  question so I want to do this justice  and as is often the case with many of  the audience questions the question as  asked is not always the most important  question okay  so first I'll answer it as asked it  sounds like the person writing is  annoyed when someone says hey how are  you I'm Joe I use him in his pronouns or  whatever the case may be the person  writing says they find it annoying and  they want to know if that's transphobic  it's not I mean on principle it is not  transphobic to find that annoying  anymore than it would be annoying then  it would be trans than it would be you  know anti-religious to find it annoying  if someone announces their declares  their religion when you meet them or  whatever else in principle it is not  transphobic that being said if you  proactively don't care about addressing  people the way they want to be addressed  then you could argue that that starts to  sort of border on being transphobic in  some sense of the word but this really  comes down to what is it that's annoying  you about it are you annoyed that trans  people want to be treated like others  that's transphobic are you just annoyed  because you don't find it necessary as a  pretext to have a normal conversation  with anybody then that's not transphobic  whatsoever here's where I think this  starts to actually become an issue that  we should be talking about and I've  talked to a lot of college professors  about this my mom is a college professor  my sister goes to a school where one  year they started doing when you go  around the class on the first day  everybody announces their gender  pronouns then they stopped I think that  that is not a good idea I don't think  that it's a good idea in a college  classroom to say let's go around the  room everybody say hello tell me your  major and tell me your gender pronouns  or whatever and there's a few reasons  why I think that that's not the best  thing number one there are people who  could be in the class who are gender  non-binary or non-conforming who don't  necessarily know what the answer is to  that question and it could actually  really upset them and I've been told a  story by a professor who said you know I  I when we did this I had a student start  to cry because they were in the middle  of not necessarily a transition but a  period during which they were unsure  about what their sort of genuine gender  identity felt like and they became very  very upset now that's anecdotal but I do  think that at the at the bigger picture  level the right way that this should be  handled in school and this is the way  I've seen a lot of people do this  successfully is in advance of the class  something goes out and it says here's  how we're going to run the class here's  the attendance policy if you have either  a nickname or a gender pronoun that  would not be obvious to me from the way  your name is in the class registry let  me know in advance and I will make a  note of it period and it solves a lot of  these issues that can sometimes just  create more anxiety and put more  pressure around people than is necessary  and I've seen that done successfully by  a lot of colleagues and people that I  know and it seems to circumvent a lot of  the problems and it also seems to  circumvent what is this by some people  described just as an annoyance at  something that's not relevant not for  any transphobic reason but just because  they want to deal with people one on one  and they will they will of course treat  people the way they want to be treated  refer to them the way they want to be  treated but the idea of going around the  room and sort of identifying seems  unnecessary I want to hear from you  about it I have no problem having my  mind changed but from all of the college  professors I've spoken to the idea of  the declaration of 